<h2>Sixteen years from payment to decree</h2>
<p>The underlying facts were narrow. In April 2006 the airline paid an AAI overfly bill in US dollars where rupees were due, generating a substantial credit. AAI's own e-mail of 5 July 2007 acknowledged Rs 1,46,33,653.71 standing to the airline's credit; Rs 78,09,000 was refunded that October; and the balance of Rs 68,24,653.71 became the suit claim. AAI's defence — that the e-mail covered only the Northern Region and the balance was adjusted against Western Region dues — failed on the evidence: the High Court found that no principal amount was shown to be due for any region, and that AAI's ledgers were not proved in accordance with the rules of evidence.</p>

<h2>The limitation battle</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>6 October 2010:</strong> plaint presented — within limitation.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Registry objections:</strong> plaint returned for curable defects; re-filed 8 October.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>16 October 2010:</strong> suit registered — the date the trial court wrongly treated as institution.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>30 October 2026:</strong> High Court holds institution occurred on presentation; the cure of defects relates back.</div>
</div>
<p>The court assembled the governing authorities: <em>Vidyawati Gupta</em> on the curability of Order VI and VII defects; <em>Mannan Lal v. Chhotaka Bibi</em> and Section 149 CPC on court-fee deficiencies relating back; <em>Uday Shankar Triyar</em> on vakalatnama defects; and the Full Bench in <em>Pragati Construction Consultants</em> for the modern synthesis — a filing is non est only where the cumulative defects show it was never a genuine plaint at all. The court also declined to import the stricter timelines of the Commercial Courts Act, 2015 into an ordinary recovery suit filed in 2010.</p>

<h2>Authority to sue: the cross-objections fail</h2>
<p>AAI's cross-objections attacked the authority of the plaintiff's attorney and the suit's institution, the airline having meanwhile merged into Air Berlin and entered insolvency administration in Germany. The court held that the power of attorney, subsequent ratification and the insolvency administrator's authorisation together cured any defect, applying <em>United Bank of India v. Naresh Kumar</em> — pleadings signed under a power of attorney can be ratified, and corporate technicalities should not defeat substantive claims.</p>

<h2>Why the judgment matters beyond its facts</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>For recovery practice:</strong> the institution date is the presentation date. Registry objections — court fees, vakalatnama, formatting — do not restart the limitation clock unless the original filing was a sham.</li>
  <li><strong>For defendants:</strong> an adjustment defence must be proved like any other claim — ledgers require formal proof, and a bald plea of regional set-off will not displace the creditor's acknowledged credit.</li>
  <li><strong>For interest claims:</strong> absent a contractual rate, courts fix what is reasonable; pleading 12% in the notice does not compel it.</li>
  <li><strong>For foreign plaintiffs:</strong> mergers and insolvency administrations abroad do not derail an Indian suit where authority is documented and ratified.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> File within limitation and keep the filing receipt — the presentation date, not the registration date, is the shield. When a plaint is returned with objections, cure and re-file promptly, and maintain a defect-wise record: under the Pragati Construction framework, the pattern of refiling is what separates a curable filing from a non est one.</p>
</div>
